Drain all buffered RX frames per RECV interrupt.

Sn_IR.RECV is a per-arrival event, not a buffer-fill level: clearing
it after every frame read deasserts INTn with several frames still
buffered, stranding them until the next arrival. read_frame now
clears the event only once the RX buffer reads empty, rechecks the
buffer after the clear, and reports whether more data is already
buffered; the runner re-reads instead of awaiting INTn while data is
pending, so a burst arriving between reads cannot wedge the RX path.
A failed read clears the drain state and sleeps briefly before the
retry, because the level-triggered INTn cannot throttle a
persistently failing bus. A corrupted-header discard also reports more
data pending, but unlike a failed read it advances the read pointer and
drains two bytes per pass, so it converges on the empty-buffer clear
rather than spinning; only the failing-bus path, which cannot make
progress, needs the sleep.

The runner also guards `rx_done` on `n > 0`, where it previously called it
unconditionally. A zero-length read now covers both an empty-buffer poll and
a corrupted-header discard, and neither should hand a spurious zero-length
frame up to embassy-net; only a real frame is announced.

Applies on top of embassy-net-wiznet-rx-frame-underflow.patch to
embassy-net-wiznet 0.3.0 from crates.io; the pin and the patch series
are consumed by nix/patched-crates.nix.

